RDP load balancing + high availability for the ldm xfreerdp backend
=====================================================================

Background: for a Linux-based xrdp server pool (no Windows RD Connection
Broker available), the standard approach is HAProxy in TCP mode in front
of the xrdp servers. This document covers three things people usually
need together: load balancing across xrdp servers, high availability of
the load balancer itself, and a staging environment for validating
updates on a live pilot group before a wider rollout (a NIS2/KRITIS-style
requirement for critical infrastructure).

The example config files referenced below are in this same directory:
haproxy-rdp.cfg, keepalived-lb1.conf, keepalived-lb2.conf.


Why HAProxy TCP mode, and why "balance source"
-----------------------------------------------

HAProxy in "mode tcp" forwards bytes without parsing the RDP protocol at
all - it doesn't understand or need to understand the RDP handshake,
TLS negotiation, or the username exchanged inside it. That also means it
CANNOT route based on the RDP username: that's exchanged encrypted,
invisible to a plain TCP proxy. Anything routing on "which user is this"
would need an RDP-aware proxy, which doesn't exist for xrdp - not
something worth building from scratch.

What HAProxy CAN see is the client's source IP, which is what
"balance source" (IP-hash) uses. This matters specifically for RDP:
sessions are stateful, so a client that disconnects and reconnects (an
auto-reconnect, a network blip) needs to land back on the SAME backend
server to find its existing session - "balance leastconn" or
"roundrobin" would happily send the reconnect to a different server,
orphaning the old session. LTSP thin clients with static/DHCP-reserved
IPs are exactly the case source-IP hashing works well for. (Caveat: if
clients instead sat behind a shared NAT gateway, they'd all hash to the
same backend - not a concern for LTSP thin clients on their own LAN
addresses, but worth knowing if the network layout ever changes.)

Verified live (see the ltsp6/ldm-mabutz development history for the full
walkthrough): session stickiness per client, automatic failover when a
backend goes down, and a real xrdp server reachable end-to-end through
the proxy with a full RDP/TLS handshake.


Staging: a defined client group tests updates before everyone else
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Since HAProxy can't distinguish RDP users, staging routing has to happen
before the RDP layer even starts - i.e. based on which physical client
connects where. Two ways to do that; the recommended one uses ldm's own
existing per-client configuration instead of adding a second place to
maintain a client list:

  A) HAProxy ACL on client source IP:
       acl is_staging_client src 10.0.0.50 10.0.0.51 10.0.0.52
       use_backend rdp_staging if is_staging_client
       default_backend rdp_production
     One shared backend pool config, an ACL list to maintain in HAProxy.

  B) Per-client lts.conf (recommended): the pilot group's thin clients
     get RDP_SERVER=<VIP_STAGING> in their lts.conf (by MAC or hostname,
     same mechanism you already use for other per-client settings),
     everyone else gets RDP_SERVER=<VIP_PROD>. Two entirely separate
     HAProxy frontends/backends (see haproxy-rdp.cfg: rdp_prod_in /
     rdp_staging_in), no shared ACL list to keep in sync elsewhere.
     Cleaner for compliance documentation too: staging vs. production is
     a clear, separately-configured environment, not a routing rule
     buried in the load balancer.

haproxy-rdp.cfg implements B: two frontends (VIP_PROD, VIP_STAGING), each
with its own backend pool. Roll an update out to the staging backend
pool first, validate with the pilot group, then apply it to the
production pool.


High availability of the load balancer itself
-------------------------------------------------

HAProxy is otherwise a single point of failure: if its host dies, every
xrdp server behind it becomes unreachable regardless of how healthy they
are. Two complementary mechanisms, worth combining rather than choosing
one:

  - keepalived + VRRP: two VMs (lb1, lb2), each running the identical
    haproxy-rdp.cfg plus keepalived. keepalived moves a floating virtual
    IP (actually two here - VIP_PROD and VIP_STAGING, moved together)
    between the two nodes. Failover time: well under a second, because
    the second HAProxy instance is already running and just starts
    responding on the VIP.

  - Proxmox HA: put both lb1 and lb2 VMs under Proxmox's own HA manager,
    on an anti-affinity rule so they never land on the same physical
    node. This protects against a Proxmox node itself dying - but the
    failover time is much longer (Proxmox has to detect the node is gone,
    fence it, then boot the VM elsewhere: commonly 30s-2min), during
    which any RDP sessions on that node are hard-disconnected.

Combining both: keepalived's VRRP failover handles "haproxy died but the
VM/host is fine" (sub-second), Proxmox HA's node-level failover handles
"the whole physical host died" (slower, but keepalived's fast path
doesn't apply there anyway since the VM itself is gone). Proxmox HA
alone, without keepalived, is a reasonable simplification if the longer
failover gap during an actual host failure is acceptable.

keepalived config notes (see keepalived-lb1.conf/keepalived-lb2.conf for
the full, commented versions) - three real keepalived 2.x gotchas that
are easy to get wrong and fail silently or behave unexpectedly:

  - A vrrp_script's "weight" is ADDED to priority on success, not
    subtracted on failure - for a "reduce priority when unhealthy" check
    (which is what you want here), it must be a NEGATIVE number, and
    large enough to bring the master's priority below the backup's
    priority (e.g. master 150, backup 100, weight -60 -> 90 < 100).
  - The config directive is "enable_script_security", not
    "script_security" (an easy typo that keepalived accepts as an
    "unknown keyword" without failing the whole config load).
  - Add "user root" to the vrrp_script block - keepalived's default
    script user (keepalived_script) doesn't exist on a plain Debian
    install, so the script's real exit status is masked as a "SECURITY
    VIOLATION" unless a valid user is set explicitly.


Proxmox HA setup for lb1/lb2
---------------------------------

NOTE: this section wasn't verified against a live Proxmox cluster (unlike
everything else in this document, which was tested end-to-end) - the
mechanism below (HA groups with per-node priority) has been stable across
PVE versions for years, but double-check exact syntax against `man
ha-manager` / the PVE documentation for your specific version before
relying on it, and test an actual failover in a maintenance window before
trusting it in production.

Goal: lb1 and lb2 normally run on two different Proxmox nodes, and
Proxmox's own HA manager restarts either one elsewhere if its node dies
(on top of, not instead of, the keepalived VRRP failover above - see the
"combining both" note above for why you want both).

1. Create the two VMs (e.g. VMIDs 200 and 201) and install them as
   described elsewhere in this document, one on each of two physical
   nodes - say "pve-a" and "pve-b".

2. Create one HA group per node, each restricted to just that node,
   and add each VM to the group for the node it normally lives on:

     ha-manager groupadd lb-pve-a --nodes pve-a
     ha-manager groupadd lb-pve-b --nodes pve-b

     ha-manager add vm:200 --group lb-pve-a --state started
     ha-manager add vm:201 --group lb-pve-b --state started

   Leaving groups unrestricted (the default) means Proxmox HA will still
   migrate a VM to *some* available node if its preferred one is down,
   rather than refusing to start it - which is what you want for actual
   host-failure recovery. The practical effect: normally lb1/lb2 stay on
   separate nodes (matching keepalived's assumption that a "node down"
   event only ever takes out one of them at a time); if pve-a is down for
   an extended period, lb1 may end up sharing pve-b with lb2 until pve-a
   comes back - a temporarily reduced but still working setup, not a
   silent failure.

3. Newer Proxmox VE versions (8.x+) additionally have a proper HA
   resource-affinity mechanism that can enforce true anti-affinity (never
   co-locate, even during a node failure) - look for "HA -> Resources ->
   Rules" in the datacenter GUI, or `/etc/pve/ha/rules.cfg` on the CLI
   side, on your specific PVE version. This is a genuinely stronger
   guarantee than step 2 above, but check your version's exact syntax
   first (this feature is newer and less universally documented than the
   classic HA-groups mechanism in step 2, which is why step 2 is the
   default recommendation here).

4. Test it: while both VMs are healthy and on separate nodes, put the
   node currently running lb1 (the active VRRP master) into maintenance
   mode (or just power it off in a lab/test setting) and confirm both
   that Proxmox restarts lb1's VM on the other available node, and that
   keepalived on lb2 has already taken over the VIPs in the meantime (the
   VRRP failover should happen in well under the time Proxmox needs to
   even detect the node is gone).


Deployment checklist
-------------------------

1. Two small VMs (lb1, lb2) on different Proxmox nodes, each also
   covered by Proxmox HA (see "Proxmox HA setup for lb1/lb2" above).
2. Install haproxy and keepalived on both.
3. Deploy haproxy-rdp.cfg (identical on both nodes) - fill in the real
   VIP_PROD/VIP_STAGING addresses and backend server IPs.
4. Deploy keepalived-lb1.conf on lb1, keepalived-lb2.conf on lb2 - fill
   in the real interface name, unicast_src_ip/unicast_peer addresses,
   and match the virtual_ipaddress block to the same VIPs used in step 3.
5. In lts.conf: RDP_SERVER=<VIP_PROD> by default; the pilot/staging
   client group gets RDP_SERVER=<VIP_STAGING> instead (by MAC/hostname).
6. Validate: pull a backend out of the pool (stop xrdp on it) and
   confirm HAProxy's stats page (port 8404) marks it down and traffic
   keeps flowing via the others; then stop haproxy on whichever node
   currently holds the VIPs and confirm the other node picks them up
   within a second or two (`ip addr show` on the VM's NIC).
